== The Biological Perspective

I didn't write about the debates in my last reflection yet I'm really looking
forward to participating in debate. I think that many of our topics will be
contentious, especially when there exists conflicting research, and I'm excited
to see what opinions we take away.

In our class discussions we talked about cases of hydrocephalus where the
swelling of the ventricles in the brain have caused the white matter to be
restricted to the edges of the cavity—yet afflicted individuals are still able
to carry on normal functions even with severely limited brain matter, which is
an incredible testament to the ability of the brain to adapt. Our discussion of
split personality disorder was also fascinating and like many mental disorders,
difficult to understand and empathize with because it is so unlike anything
that neurotypical individuals experience. I hope that in this class, I'll learn
a lot to hopefully understand even a fraction of what they experience.

As for the readings, most sounded super familiar: in anatomy, we also discussed
the specific glial cells—but what stood out to me was how we didn't know that
much about microglia (which the book doesn't really talk about). In class and
in the book the topic of electroconvulsive therapy was brought up; I didn't
know it was used to treat depression because I'd always heard about its use for
conversion therapy rather than a useful remedy, so understanding its medicinal
applications was intriguing. The later parts about split brain disorder remind
me of what you said on the first day—how we get to understand these systems
only when they go wrong.
